-- statement:
--   The theorem states that there exist real sequences θ and c (indexed by the natural numbers) forming a counterexample realization in the infinite-product model over the base space of sequences of points of the unit circle ℝ/ℤ with the infinite product of Haar probability measure on each coordinate. By definition this means five things hold. First, the angles and costs are admissible: every θ_i lies strictly between 0 and 1, every c_i is positive, the series of θ_i is summable, and 1 together with all the θ_i is linearly independent over ℚ. Second, θ has all finite-coordinate sampling: for every finite set I of coordinates, the triangular law on pairs of base points (the restriction of the product measure to the event that x_i < y_i in the representatives in [0,1) for all i in I, with coordinates outside I identified) is a weak limit of finite measures η_l that are each supported on ordered pairs (x, y) with y equal to x shifted by n·θ coordinatewise for some n in a fixed finite set of positive integers, and whose two marginals have densities with uniformly bounded L² norm. Third, the weight w(x) = exp(−Σ_i c_i ⌊x_i + θ_i⌋) is positive for almost every base point. Fourth, the integral of the negative logarithm of w is infinite. Fifth, the weighted shift operator (the shift on the ℤ-indexed ℓ² fibre composed with multiplication by w along rotation orbits) satisfies the product operator properties: the von Neumann algebra generated by the shift and bounded measurable orbit multipliers is a type II₁ factor with the given trace vector and separable predual, the operator lies in it, is nonzero, is norm-quasinilpotent, has vector Fuglede–Kadison determinant 0, has spectrum {0}, has the Dirac mass at 0 as its Brown measure, has only trivial invariant projections in the factor, and has a polar decomposition with unitary shift and injective modulus having the stated atomic and abelian diffuse polar-algebra structure.
